  only writes app state.
- triggerEvent is normally click, change, or submit.
- actionType must be one of: ${behaviorVocabulary.join(", ")}.
- One handwritten note may justify MANY edges. "digits append to the display"
  over a keypad means one edge per digit button. Emit them all.
- tests are short behavioral assertions, not executable test files.
- If a mark is ambiguous, omit it. Never invent UI or wiring that is not drawn.

## Handler contract — read carefully

handler is a JavaScript statement body executed in the browser. It is REAL code
that runs; it is not a description. These names are in scope and nothing else:

  state          plain object of all current app state
  get(key)       read one state value
  set(key, val)  write one state value
  event          { type, value, componentId } — value is the typed text for
                 inputs, or the button label for clicks
  self           the component that fired
  target         the target component, or null
  num(v)         coerce anything to a finite number (blank/garbage becomes 0)

No imports, no await, no fetch, no DOM, no timers, no console. Do not declare a
function — write the statements directly. Multiple handlers triggered by the
same event run in order against the same state, and the UI re-renders once.

Write every string literal in handler code with SINGLE quotes. A handler is
carried inside a JSON string, and single quotes keep that escaping trivial.

initialState declares the starting values every handler can rely on. Return it
as key/value pairs; values are strings and handlers coerce with num() as needed.

## Worked example — an arithmetic calculator

Given a display box and drawn keys, correct output looks like:

component: { id: "display", type: "Text", stateKey: "display", label: "0", … }
component: { id: "key7", type: "Button", stateKey: "", label: "7", … }

initialState: [ {key:"display",value:"0"}, {key:"acc",value:"0"},
